<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Kedatuan of Madja-as</h1><p class="st-meta">1200 CE – 1569 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A confederation of Visayan settlements on Panay, known from the legend of ten Bornean datus who bought the island from the Ati people.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The story of Madja-as comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maragtas">Maragtas</a>, published by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-monteclaro">Pedro Monteclaro</a> in 1907 from oral tradition. It tells of ten datus fleeing a tyrant in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borneo">Borneo</a>, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/datu-puti">Datu Puti</a>, who bought the lowlands of Panay from the Ati chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marikudo">Marikudo</a> for a golden salakot, a native hat, and a long gold necklace. Historians such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-henry-scott">William Henry Scott</a> regard the account as folklore, not history.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Panay's real 16th-century society, described by the Spanish, was made up of independent coastal communities led by datus, with nobles, freemen and dependants. They grew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, traded with Chinese and Bornean merchants and practised ancestor worship.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miguel-lopez-de-legazpi">Miguel Lopez de Legazpi</a> moved his base to Panay in 1569 and Spanish rule followed. The Madja-as legend is now celebrated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ati-atihan-festival">Ati-Atihan</a> and Binirayan festivals and remains part of Visayan identity.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kedatuan-of-madja-as.jpg" alt="Kedatuan of Madja-as" width="320" height="314"><figcaption>Image: Delirium333 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Confederation_of_Madjaas.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1200 CE – 1569 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Indigenous Philippine religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Kinaray-a, Hiligaynon</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/philippines">Philippines</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Madja-as, Confederation of Madja-as</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1384&amp;focus=kedatuan-of-madja-as" aria-label="Kedatuan of Madja-as on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8052 1752 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kedatuan of Madja-as lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8309 1890h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8372 2008h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M8372 2008h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Philippines. <a href="/atlas?year=1384&amp;focus=kedatuan-of-madja-as">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

